    "summary": "Strong first set with Jack Straw & Althea, second set uneven.",
    "blurb": "The show opens tightly with Let It Grow, Jack Straw, Althea and Queen Jane Approximately, delivering solid jams on Loser and Tennessee Jed. Highlights include a fiery Truckin' > New Speedway Boogie transition and an emotional Standing On The Moon in the second set, though the latter half drags with weaker performances of Uncle John’s Band and Corrina.",
    "review": "July 13, 1994 at Franklin County Airport captures a Grateful Dead night that swings between brilliance and stumble. The first set is a bright spot – Jerry’s lead work on Jack Straw shines with a “phenomenal” tone, Althea rolls out smoothly, and the band locks into Let It Grow and Tennessee Jed with crisp, energetic playing. The soundboard recording praised as “great soundboard” captures these moments cleanly, despite occasional speaker drop‑outs.\n\nThe second set is more mixed. A high‑octane Truckin' > New Speedway Boogie transition fuels the crowd, and Jerry’s eye‑contact during Standing On The Moon moves listeners to tears, but the momentum fizzles as Uncle John’s Band suffers lyrical flubs and Corrina drags. Vince Welnick’s keyboard antics draw mixed reactions – some fans love his shirt‑off stage presence while others hear him out of sync. Overall, the show offers enough standout moments (Jack Straw, Althea, Standing On The Moon) to merit a listen, especially via the recommended soundboard source.",
